WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:26.920
Good evening

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:30.120
Good evening

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:31.840
I'm Brian Greene

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.780
In April 1919

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:39.740
a 34-year-old German Mathematician

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.640
was in his study

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:42.600
sitting on his desk

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.840
His 9 year old son was on the couch

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:46.980
were often being with daddy's working

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:47.680
and

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:50.140
Theodor Kaluza was there at his desk

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:52.540
doing calculation as he often would

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.060
when all the sudden

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.540
he opened his eyes wide

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:58.540
he slammed the desk with his hands

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:00.540
he shot to his feet

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.920
and he began to sing an Aria from Figaro

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.800
The program here tonight

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:35.720
will explore what was the Theodor Kaluza

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:37.560
was singing about

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:40.000
he had found the possibility

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:43.920
that there is reason to believe that our universe might have

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.360
more than the three dimensions

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:49.120
that everyday space that we all know about

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:51.400
the problem that will be the discussion tonight

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.400
will follow the variations on that theme

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.400
that have rippled through physics

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:58.200
over the last eight decades

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:01.800
and we' ll discuss where our thinking is today on this possibility

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:06.080
of more dimensions than meet the eye

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:10.880
now, to give you a sense of where this idea came from

00:15:16.420 --> 00:15:17.220
Calabi-Yau

